<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="activity-derivatives-trigonometric-functions">
  <title>Derivatives of Trigonometric Functions</title>

  <objectives xml:id="obj-derivatives-trigonometric-functions">
    <ul>
      <li><p>Compare the areas of two triangles and a circular sector on the unit circle to bound <m>\dfrac{\sin(\theta)}{\theta}</m>, and use the Squeeze Theorem to find its limit as <m>\theta\to 0</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Explain why radian measure, where a full turn is <m>\tau=2\pi</m>, is the natural way to measure angles in calculus.</p></li>
      <li><p>Find <m>\displaystyle\lim_{\theta\to 0}\dfrac{\cos(\theta)-1}{\theta}</m> by multiplying by a conjugate, using a Pythagorean identity, and factoring out a known limit.</p></li>
      <li><p>Use the two limits to obtain <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\sin(x)</m> and <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\cos(x)</m>, and differentiate expressions that combine sine and cosine with the product rule and the chain rule.</p></li>
      <li><p>Use the quotient rule to find the derivatives of the other four trigonometric functions, and apply them together with the chain rule.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Last time the sum identities reduced the derivatives of sine and cosine to two limits,
      <m>A=\displaystyle\lim_{h\to 0}\dfrac{\cos(h)-1}{h}</m> and
      <m>B=\displaystyle\lim_{h\to 0}\dfrac{\sin(h)}{h}</m> (see <xref ref="ex-td-which-limits"/>).
      Both limits have the form <m>\frac{0}{0}</m>, and we cannot use L'H&#244;pital's rule to find
      them, because that rule needs the derivative of sine, which is exactly what we are trying to
      find. Instead we return to the unit circle. Today's work will also show us which units we
      should use to measure angles.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-dt-compare-regions" workspace="0in">
    <title>Comparing Three Regions</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The figure shows the first quadrant of the unit circle and an acute angle <m>\theta</m>.
        Three regions are shaded: a small triangle with height <m>\sin(\theta)</m>, the sector of
        the circle cut off by the angle <m>\theta</m>, and a large triangle with height
        <m>\tan(\theta)</m>. Both triangles have a base of length <m>1</m>, the radius of the circle.
      </p>
      <image width="30%">
        <shortdescription>
          The first quadrant of the unit circle. A point P on the circle at the angle theta above the
          x-axis has the coordinates cosine of theta, sine of theta. A dashed vertical segment from P
          to the x-axis is labeled sine of theta. The point S is 1, 0 on the x-axis, the point O is the
          origin, and the base from O to S is labeled 1. The ray from O through P continues to the
          point Q on the vertical line through S, and the segment from S up to Q is labeled tangent of
          theta. The small triangle with vertices O, S, and P is shaded darkly. The sector between the
          x-axis and the ray, bounded by the circle, is shaded lightly. The large triangle with
          vertices O, S, and Q is shaded in gray and contains the sector.
        </shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=5, &gt;=stealth]
            \pgfmathsetmacro{\a}{40}
            \pgfmathsetmacro{\px}{cos(\a)}
            \pgfmathsetmacro{\py}{sin(\a)}
            \pgfmathsetmacro{\qy}{tan(\a)}
            \fill[gray!20] (0,0) -- (1,0) -- (1,\qy) -- cycle;
            \fill[blue!20] (0,0) -- (1,0) arc (0:\a:1) -- cycle;
            \fill[blue!45] (0,0) -- (1,0) -- (\px,\py) -- cycle;
            \draw[-&gt;] (-0.05,0) -- (1.25,0) node[right] {$x$};
            \draw[-&gt;] (0,-0.05) -- (0,1.1) node[above] {$y$};
            \draw (1,0) arc (0:90:1);
            \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (1,\qy) -- (1,0);
            \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (1,0);
            \draw[thick, blue!70!black] (1,0) arc (0:\a:1);
            \draw[dashed] (\px,\py) -- (\px,0);
            \fill (0,0) circle (0.012);
            \fill (1,0) circle (0.012);
            \fill (\px,\py) circle (0.012);
            \fill (1,\qy) circle (0.012);
            \node[below left] at (0,0) {$O$};
            \node[below right] at (1,0) {$S$};
            \node[above left] at (\px,\py) {$P$};
            \node[above right] at (1,\qy) {$Q$};
            \draw (0.2,0) arc (0:\a:0.2);
            \node at (0.29,0.08) {$\theta$};
            \node[below] at (0.5,0) {$1$};
            \node[left] at (\px,{\py/2}) {$\sin(\theta)$};
            \node[right] at (1,{\qy/2}) {$\tan(\theta)$};
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
      <p>
        Let <m>\tau</m> be the measure of one full turn around the circle. If angles are measured in
        degrees then <m>\tau=360</m>, and if angles are measured in radians then
        <m>\tau=2\pi</m>. In either case, <m>\dfrac{\theta}{\tau}</m> is the fraction of the circle
        covered by the rotation through the angle <m>\theta</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-dt-triangle-areas" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the area of the small triangle and the area of the large triangle, using
          <m>\frac{1}{2}\cdot\text{base}\cdot\text{height}</m> and the heights marked in the figure.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-dt-sector-area" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The unit circle has area <m>\pi</m>. Explain why the area of the sector is
          <m>\pi\cdot\dfrac{\theta}{\tau}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-dt-area-inequality" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Look at how the three regions sit inside one another in the figure. Use this to write an
          inequality that orders the three areas from smallest to largest, in terms of
          <m>\sin(\theta)</m>, <m>\theta</m>, <m>\tau</m>, and <m>\tan(\theta)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-dt-squeeze">
    <title>Squeeze Theorem</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>g(x)\le f(x)\le h(x)</m> for all <m>x</m> near <m>a</m> (except possibly at
        <m>x=a</m>), and suppose <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x\to a}g(x)=L=\lim_{x\to a}h(x)</m>. Then
        <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x\to a}f(x)=L</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-dt-squeezing-sine">
    <title>Squeezing <m>\sin(\theta)/\theta</m></title>
    <p>
      The inequality from the last exercise is
      <md>
        <mrow> \frac{1}{2}\sin(\theta) \lt \pi\cdot\frac{\theta}{\tau} \lt \frac{1}{2}\tan(\theta)\text{,} </mrow>
      </md>
      for <m>0\lt\theta\lt\frac{\tau}{4}</m>. We want <m>\dfrac{\sin(\theta)}{\theta}</m> in the middle.
      Because <m>\theta\gt 0</m>, we may multiply the left inequality by <m>\dfrac{2}{\theta}</m>
      without changing its direction, which gives
      <m>\dfrac{\sin(\theta)}{\theta}\lt\dfrac{2\pi}{\tau}</m>.
      In the right inequality we replace <m>\tan(\theta)</m> by
      <m>\dfrac{\sin(\theta)}{\cos(\theta)}</m>. Because <m>\cos(\theta)\gt 0</m> as well, multiplying by
      <m>\dfrac{2\cos(\theta)}{\theta}</m> gives
      <m>\dfrac{2\pi}{\tau}\cos(\theta)\lt\dfrac{\sin(\theta)}{\theta}</m>. Together,
      <md>
        \frac{2\pi}{\tau}\cos(\theta)\lt\frac{\sin(\theta)}{\theta}\lt\frac{2\pi}{\tau}\text{.}
      </md>
      As <m>\theta\to 0^+</m>, the point on the circle approaches <m>(1,0)</m>, so
      <m>\cos(\theta)\to 1</m>. Both outer expressions then approach <m>\dfrac{2\pi}{\tau}</m>, and
      <xref ref="thm-dt-squeeze"/> tells us that
      <md>
        \lim_{\theta\to 0^+}\frac{\sin(\theta)}{\theta}=\frac{2\pi}{\tau}\text{.}
      </md>
      The argument used <m>\theta\gt 0</m>, but sine is odd (see <xref ref="thm-uc-odd-even"/>), so
      <m>\dfrac{\sin(-\theta)}{-\theta}=\dfrac{\sin(\theta)}{\theta}</m>. The values for negative
      <m>\theta</m> match the values for positive <m>\theta</m>, so the limit from the left is the
      same, and <m>\displaystyle\lim_{\theta\to 0}\dfrac{\sin(\theta)}{\theta}=\dfrac{2\pi}{\tau}</m>.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-dt-which-units" workspace="0in">
    <title>Choosing the Units for Angles</title>
    <task xml:id="task-dt-degrees-limit" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose we measure angles in degrees. What is <m>\displaystyle\lim_{\theta\to 0}\dfrac{\sin(\theta)}{\theta}</m>?
          Give the exact value and a decimal approximation.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-dt-natural-units" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which value of <m>\tau</m> makes this limit equal to <m>1</m>? Why would that choice make
          the derivative of sine simpler to write down?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-dt-radians">
    <title>Radians</title>
    <p>
      An angle is measured in <term>radians</term> when a full turn is <m>\tau=2\pi</m>. Then
      <m>\dfrac{2\pi}{\tau}=1</m>, and the arc of the unit circle covered by a rotation through
      <m>\theta</m> has length <m>\dfrac{\theta}{2\pi}\cdot 2\pi=\theta</m>. So the radian measure of an angle
      is the length of the arc it cuts off on the unit circle. With this choice,
      <md>
        \lim_{\theta\to 0}\frac{\sin(\theta)}{\theta}=1\text{.}
      </md>
      From now on, all angles are measured in radians.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-dt-cosine-limit" workspace="0in">
    <title>The Limit of <m>(\cos(\theta)-1)/\theta</m></title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        We now find the second limit, using <m>\displaystyle\lim_{\theta\to 0}\dfrac{\sin(\theta)}{\theta}=1</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-dt-conjugate" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Multiply the numerator and the denominator of <m>\dfrac{\cos(\theta)-1}{\theta}</m> by the
          conjugate <m>\cos(\theta)+1</m>. Use the Pythagorean identity
          <m>\sin^2(\theta)+\cos^2(\theta)=1</m> to show that
          <md>
            \frac{\cos(\theta)-1}{\theta}=\frac{-\sin^2(\theta)}{\theta\,(\cos(\theta)+1)}\text{.}
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-dt-factor-product" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write the right side as a product of <m>\dfrac{\sin(\theta)}{\theta}</m> and one other factor
          that contains <m>\sin(\theta)</m> and <m>\cos(\theta)</m> but no <m>\theta</m> in a
          denominator.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-dt-product-limit" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the limit of a product to find <m>\displaystyle\lim_{\theta\to 0}\dfrac{\cos(\theta)-1}{\theta}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-dt-two-limits">
    <title>The Two Fundamental Limits</title>
    <p>
      For angles measured in radians,
      <md>
        <mrow> \lim_{\theta\to 0}\frac{\sin(\theta)}{\theta} \amp = 1 \amp \lim_{\theta\to 0}\frac{\cos(\theta)-1}{\theta} \amp = 0\text{.} </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-dt-derivative-sin-cos" workspace="0in">
    <title>Derivatives of Sine and Cosine</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Using the sum identities and the definition of the derivative, last time we wrote
        <md>
          <mrow> \frac{d}{dx}\sin(x) \amp = \sin(x)\cdot\lim_{h\to 0}\frac{\cos(h)-1}{h}+\cos(x)\cdot\lim_{h\to 0}\frac{\sin(h)}{h} </mrow>
          <mrow> \frac{d}{dx}\cos(x) \amp = \cos(x)\cdot\lim_{h\to 0}\frac{\cos(h)-1}{h}-\sin(x)\cdot\lim_{h\to 0}\frac{\sin(h)}{h}\text{.} </mrow>
        </md>
        Now we know the value of both limits.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-dt-sine-derivative" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Substitute the values of the two limits to find <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\sin(x)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-dt-cosine-derivative" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Substitute the values of the two limits to find <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\cos(x)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-dt-sine-cosine-derivatives">
    <title>Derivatives of Sine and Cosine</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        When <m>x</m> is measured in radians,
        <md>
          <mrow> \frac{d}{dx}\sin(x) \amp = \cos(x) \amp \frac{d}{dx}\cos(x) \amp = -\sin(x)\text{.} </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-dt-practice-sine-cosine" workspace="0in">
    <title>Practice with Sine and Cosine</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Use <xref ref="thm-dt-sine-cosine-derivatives"/> together with the product rule and the
        chain rule to find each derivative.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-dt-practice-cos-four" workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p><m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\bigl[\cos(4x)\bigr]</m></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-dt-practice-x-squared-sine" workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p><m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\bigl[x^2\sin(x)\bigr]</m></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-dt-practice-sine-cubed" workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p><m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\bigl[\sin^3(x)\bigr]</m></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-dt-practice-exp-cosine" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p><m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\bigl[e^{-x}\cos(2x)\bigr]</m></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-dt-example-tangent">
    <title>Example: The Derivative of Tangent</title>
    <p>
      The other four trigonometric functions are quotients involving sine and cosine (see
      <xref ref="def-uc-trig-functions"/>), so we can find their derivatives with the quotient rule. For
      tangent, <m>\tan(x)=\dfrac{\sin(x)}{\cos(x)}</m>, and
      <md>
        <mrow> \frac{d}{dx}\tan(x) \amp = \frac{d}{dx}\left[\frac{\sin(x)}{\cos(x)}\right] = \frac{\cos(x)\cdot\sin'(x)-\sin(x)\cdot\cos'(x)}{\cos^2(x)} </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp = \frac{\cos(x)\cdot\cos(x)-\sin(x)\cdot\bigl(-\sin(x)\bigr)}{\cos^2(x)} </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp = \frac{\cos^2(x)+\sin^2(x)}{\cos^2(x)} = \frac{1}{\cos^2(x)} = \sec^2(x)\text{.} </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-dt-derivative-secant" workspace="0in">
    <title>The Derivative of Secant</title>
    <task xml:id="task-dt-secant-quotient" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write <m>\sec(x)=\dfrac{1}{\cos(x)}</m> and use the quotient rule (or the chain rule
          applied to <m>\bigl(\cos(x)\bigr)^{-1}</m>) to find <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\sec(x)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-dt-secant-rewrite" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Rewrite your answer as a product of two of the functions <m>\sec(x)</m>, <m>\tan(x)</m>,
          <m>\csc(x)</m>, and <m>\cot(x)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-dt-trig-derivative-rules">
    <title>Derivatives of the Trigonometric Functions</title>
    <p>
      With <m>x</m> in radians, at every <m>x</m> where the function is defined,
      <md>
        <mrow> \frac{d}{dx}\sin(x) \amp = \cos(x) \amp \frac{d}{dx}\cos(x) \amp = -\sin(x) </mrow>
        <mrow> \frac{d}{dx}\tan(x) \amp = \sec^2(x) \amp \frac{d}{dx}\cot(x) \amp = -\csc^2(x) </mrow>
        <mrow> \frac{d}{dx}\sec(x) \amp = \sec(x)\tan(x) \amp \frac{d}{dx}\csc(x) \amp = -\csc(x)\cot(x)\text{.} </mrow>
      </md>
      The derivatives of cotangent and cosecant follow from the quotient rule in the same way as
      tangent and secant. Notice that the derivative of each <q>co</q> function is negative.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-dt-practice-trig-chain" workspace="0in">
    <title>Practice with All Six Functions</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Use the rules in <xref ref="assemblage-dt-trig-derivative-rules" text="title"/>, along with
        the chain rule, product rule, and quotient rule as needed, to find each derivative.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-dt-practice-sec-four" workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p><m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\bigl[\sec(4x)\bigr]</m></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-dt-practice-exp-tan" workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p><m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\bigl[e^{\tan(x)}\bigr]</m></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-dt-practice-x-cot" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p><m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\bigl[x^2\cot(x)\bigr]</m></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-dt-practice-ln-sec" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\bigl[\ln\bigl|\sec(x)\bigr|\bigr]</m>.
          Simplify your answer as much as you can.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
